{
  "pages": [
    {
      "page_idx": 5,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.099,
            0.104,
            0.17,
            0.12
          ],
          "content": "CIRCLES"
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "bbox": [
            0.863,
            0.104,
            0.899,
            0.119
          ],
          "content": "149"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.094,
            0.139,
            0.562,
            0.178
          ],
          "content": "**Theorem 10.2 :** The lengths of tangents drawn from an external point to a circle are equal."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.095,
            0.184,
            0.564,
            0.262
          ],
          "content": "**Proof : We are given a circle with centre O, a point P lying outside the circle and two tangents PQ, PR on the circle from P (see Fig. 10.7). We** are required to prove that $\\mathrm { P Q } = \\mathrm { P R }$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.096,
            0.266,
            0.564,
            0.364
          ],
          "content": "**For this, we join OP, OQ and OR. Then** $\\angle \\mathrm { O Q P }$ **and** $\\angle  ORP$ **are right angles, because these are angles between the radii and tangents, and according to Theorem 10.1 they are right angles. Now in right triangles OQP and ORP,**"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.596,
            0.163,
            0.89,
            0.305
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [
            {
              "bbox": [
                0.699,
                0.323,
                0.8,
                0.344
              ],
              "content": "Fig. 10.7"
            },
            {
              "bbox": [
                0.652,
                0.366,
                0.898,
                0.385
              ],
              "content": "(Radii of the same circle)"
            }
          ],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_5_image_5.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.429,
            0.366,
            0.538,
            0.385
          ],
          "content": "\\mathrm { O Q = O R }",
          "image_source": "images/page_5_equation_6.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.434,
            0.39,
            0.537,
            0.409
          ],
          "content": "\\mathrm { O P } = \\mathrm { O P }",
          "image_source": "images/page_5_equation_8.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.791,
            0.391,
            0.898,
            0.411
          ],
          "content": "**(Common)**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.098,
            0.416,
            0.205,
            0.435
          ],
          "content": "**Therefore,**"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.395,
            0.415,
            0.897,
            0.435
          ],
          "content": "\\Delta \\mathrm { \\scriptsize ~ O Q P } \\cong \\Delta \\mathrm { \\scriptsize ~ O R P }\\tag{RHS}",
          "image_source": "images/page_5_equation_12.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.098,
            0.442,
            0.202,
            0.462
          ],
          "content": "**This gives**"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.432,
            0.44,
            0.898,
            0.462
          ],
          "content": "\\mathrm{PQ} = \\mathrm{PR}\\tag{CPCT}",
          "image_source": "images/page_5_equation_14.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.098,
            0.486,
            0.203,
            0.504
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Remarks"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.098,
            0.511,
            0.865,
            0.531
          ],
          "content": "**1. The theorem can also be proved by using the Pythagoras Theorem as follows:**"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.146,
            0.535,
            0.682,
            0.555
          ],
          "content": "\\mathrm{PQ}^{2}=\\mathrm{OP}^{2}-\\mathrm{O} \\mathrm{Q}^{2}=\\mathrm{OP}^{2}-\\mathrm{OR}^{2}=\\mathrm{PR}^{2} \\text { (As OQ=OR) }",
          "image_source": "images/page_5_equation_17.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.098,
            0.561,
            0.318,
            0.581
          ],
          "content": "**which gives PQ = PR.**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.096,
            0.585,
            0.898,
            0.626
          ],
          "content": "**2. Note also that** $\\angle OPQ = \\angle OPR$ Therefore, OP is the angle bisector of $\\angle \\mathrm { Q P R }$ **i.e., the centre lies on the bisector of the angle between the two tangents.**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.097,
            0.632,
            0.37,
            0.651
          ],
          "content": "**Let us take some examples.**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.096,
            0.663,
            0.562,
            0.722
          ],
          "content": "**Example 1 : Prove that in two concentric circles, the chord of the larger circle, which touches the smaller circle, is bisected at the point of contact.**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.096,
            0.727,
            0.563,
            0.823
          ],
          "content": "**Solution : We are given two concentric circles** $\\mathbf { C } _ { \\mathrm { i } }$ and $\\mathbf { C } _ { 2 }$ **with centre O and a chord AB of the** larger circle $\\mathrm{C}_{1}$ **which touches the smaller circle** $\\mathbf { C } _ { z }$ **at the point** $\\mathrm { P }$ **(see Fig. 10.8). We need to prove** that $\\mathrm { A P } = \\mathrm { B P } .$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.096,
            0.829,
            0.563,
            0.868
          ],
          "content": "Let us join OP. Then, AB is a tangent to $\\mathbf { C } _ { 2 }$ **at P and OP is its radius. Therefore, by Theorem 10.1,**"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.255,
            0.874,
            0.362,
            0.893
          ],
          "content": "\\mathrm { O P \\perp A B }",
          "image_source": "images/page_5_equation_24.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.615,
            0.669,
            0.858,
            0.821
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [
            {
              "bbox": [
                0.687,
                0.833,
                0.786,
                0.854
              ],
              "content": "Fig. 10.8"
            }
          ],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_5_image_25.jpg"
        },
